---
functor: 1
title: Notation check and trace
---

set heading(numbering: none)

The compiler keeps a trace for each symbol. The trace shows the four layers of the symbol: meaning, style, construction and glyph. The inspector and the notation check use the same trace.

# The trace of one symbol

| Layer | Question | Example for `v` after `object vec: v` |
|:--|:--|:--|
| Meaning | What is it? | `v` is a vector, from `object vec: v`. |
| Style | Which rule sets its look? | `set vec(style: arrow)`, the default |
| Construction | Which recipe makes it? | `v.arrow`, and the meaning of each operation |
| Glyph | Where does the outline come from? | a font glyph, a font composite, or a built glyph from the cache |

# See a trace

In the editor and the desktop app, put the cursor on a symbol. The **Symbol** panel shows its four layers and the glyph.

The **Symbol** panel and the **Problems** panel are dock panels. Each panel has three buttons in its title bar. One button moves the panel between the bottom of the editor and the side of the window. One button opens the panel in its own window. One button hides the panel. The **Problems** and **Symbol** buttons in the status bar show a hidden panel again. In a panel window, the dock button puts the panel back. Click a problem in a panel window to move the cursor in the editor.

In a terminal, `functor trace file.fn` prints the trace of each symbol. Add a line, or a line and a column, to see one place:

```text
$ functor trace paper.fn 9:7
9:7  norm(v)
  meaning       The kind `norm`: a norm.
  style         `set norm(fence: vbar.x2)`, the default
  construction  fence: vbar.x2
  glyph         Fence glyphs from the font. They grow to fit the body.
```

# The notation check

The check compares the traces of all symbols in the document. It finds two problems: one look with two meanings, and one meaning with two looks. Each finding is a warning with a hint in the **Problems** panel. `functor check` shows the same warnings.

| Check | You wrote | Hint |
|:--|:--|:--|
| A hand form looks the same as a meaning form | `norm(v)` and `(v).vbar.x2` | Write `norm(v)`. |
| A hand fence looks the same as a meaning name | `abs(x)` and `(y).vbar` | Write `abs(y)`. |
| A recipe looks the same as a `sym` | `sym csum = sum.circle`, then `sum.circle` | Write `csum`. |
| A hand style on a declared vector | `object vec: v`, then `v.bold` | Remove `.bold`, or change the rule for all vectors. |
| A hand style that the rule already gives | `object vec: v`, then `v.arrow` | Remove `.arrow`. |
| One letter with two vector looks | `u.arrow` and `u.bold` | Write `object vec: u` and one `set vec` rule. |
| Two meanings with one look | `set abs(fence: vbar.x2)`, then `abs(x)` and `norm(v)` | Give one of them its own look. |

A meaning form is an `object`, a `sym` or a kind call such as `norm(...)`. A hand form is a recipe or a fence that you write in place.

```fn
object vec: v

$norm(v) = (v).vbar.x2$
```

This example compiles, and the check gives a warning on `(v).vbar.x2`.

# Turn the check off

These are guidelines, not rules, so the check gives warnings only. A page that compares notations on purpose can turn the check off:

```fn
set lint(notation: off)

$abs(x) = (x).vbar$
```
